// @test: differential
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0,1
// @tags: vbc, codegen, generics, name-resolution, iterator, collect, regression
// @timeout: 60000
// @expected-exit: 0
// @expected-stdout-begin
// range=5
// mapped=5
// abi=Stdcall
// second=C
// @expected-stdout-end
//
// THE VALUES ARE NAMED, NOT ONLY THE AGREEMENT. A `differential` spec
// asks the two tiers to agree, and two tiers agree perfectly when both
// are wrong in the same way. Pre-fix Tier 1 does not reach any of these
// lines — it aborts inside `Range.collect` — but a future change that
// broke Tier 0 the same way would satisfy a bare agreement check and
// fail this one, which is the point.
//
// T1214 — a GENERIC RETURN PARAMETER loses its name to a VARIANT
// CONSTRUCTOR, and the whole of `collect()` dies at Tier 1 with it.
//
//     core/base/iterator.vr:677
//       fn collect<C: FromIterator<Self.Item>>(self) -> C { C.from_iter(self) }
//
//     core/math/internal.vr:381
//       public type FFIAbi is | C | Stdcall | Fastcall | ...
//
// `C` is `collect`'s return type parameter AND the name of `FFIAbi`'s
// first variant — the C calling convention. `extract_expr_type_name`
// resolves a bare UPPERCASE identifier by asking which sum type owns a
// variant of that name, and asks it WITHOUT first checking whether the
// name is a type parameter in scope. So the receiver of `.from_iter`
// was typed `FFIAbi`, the call was emitted as `FFIAbi.from_iter`, and
// Tier 1 refused it honestly:
//
//     PANIC: AOT dispatch fault: method 'FFIAbi.from_iter' has no
//     compiled candidate in this module
//
// THE BLAST RADIUS IS EVERY RECEIVER, not one. In the program that
// found this, the same abort was compiled into `Args.collect`,
// `ByRef.collect`, `FilterIter.collect`, `MappedIter.collect`,
// `Range.collect`, `TransducedIter.collect` and `StreamCollect.poll` —
// one generic body compiled once per receiver, mis-resolving the same
// name every time.
//
// THAT CLAIM WAS MINE AND IT WAS WRONG, so it is corrected here rather
// than quietly dropped. This header used to say the local `Abi` below
// CREATES the collision, on the reasoning that keying on `FFIAbi` would
// make the spec depend on the mount graph. Measured instead: a probe
// mounting only `core.prelude.*` and declaring NO local sum type already
// produces
//
//     in `Args.collect`: CallM method 'FFIAbi.from_iter' unresolved
//
// so `core/math/internal.vr` is in the prelude's graph and the collision
// is core's, not this file's. `Abi` therefore earns its place as a
// CONTROL and not as the subject — see the two negative controls in
// `main`, which are the half of this spec a naive fix would break.
//
// AND IT IS PROGRAM-DEPENDENT IN THE OTHER DIRECTION TOO, which is why
// it hid for so long. TWO core types declare a variant named `C` —
// `FFIAbi` and `ReprKind` (core/meta/attribute.vr). The lookup answers
// "ambiguous" and declines when BOTH are registered, so a program that
// mounts `meta.attribute` never sees this. The defect needs the
// collision to be UNIQUE in the module.
//
// WHY NOT THE EXISTING `stdlib-runtime/collect_basic.vr`: that spec is
// `typecheck-pass` at tier 0, and its own header says runtime semantics
// are not wired. It guards the inference half and cannot observe
// whether `collect` produces anything at all.

mount core.prelude.*;

// The collision, declared here so the file owns it. `C` is a perfectly
// ordinary variant name — the C calling convention, an ABI tag, a
// grade — and nothing about it should reach a generic parameter.
type Abi is | C | Stdcall;

fn main() {
    // POSITIVE — the defect itself. Pre-fix, Tier 0 prints these and
    // Tier 1 aborts inside `Range.collect` / `MappedIter.collect`.
    let nums: List<Int> = (0..5).collect();
    print(f"range={nums.len()}");

    let xs = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    let doubled: List<Int> = xs.iter().map(|n| n * 2).collect();
    print(f"mapped={doubled.len()}");

    // NEGATIVE CONTROL — the arm being guarded must keep its day job.
    // A bare variant name that is NOT a type parameter in scope still
    // has to resolve to its parent type; a fix that answered "unknown"
    // for every uppercase identifier would pass the positive half and
    // break this.
    let a = Abi.Stdcall;
    match a {
        Abi.C => print("abi=C"),
        Abi.Stdcall => print("abi=Stdcall"),
    }

    // SECOND NEGATIVE CONTROL — a variant whose name is a SINGLE
    // UPPERCASE LETTER and is genuinely wanted as a value. This is the
    // case a `looks_like_type_param` heuristic would break: it answers
    // true for any one-letter uppercase name, so it cannot tell this
    // line from the one inside `collect`. Only the per-function
    // generic-parameter SET can.
    let b = Abi.C;
    match b {
        Abi.C => print("second=C"),
        Abi.Stdcall => print("second=Stdcall"),
    }
}
